// @emit-error emitProgram: if-expression arm ends in a void call, which yields no value
// BOUNDARY PIN (message wording, not coverage): an if-expression arm whose last statement
// is a VOID call yields nothing, so the join has no value to produce, and the emitter
// rejects. The arm IS a single well-formed expression — it is void — so the message names
// VOIDNESS rather than claiming the arm is "not a single value", which is what it used to
// say and what sent a reader looking for a second statement that was never there.
//
// The LAYER this belongs to is the CHECKER, not the emitter: VL already rules a void value
// in a value slot a type error on every ANNOTATED path, and this same body with a return
// ANNOTATION is caught there instead —
//
//     function f(c: boolean): i32 { return if c { 1 } else { sideeffect() } }
//     //  ERROR: return type mismatch: expected i32, got i32 | void
//
// pinned by `error-void-if-arm-annotated-return.vl`. Only the UN-ANNOTATED inference path
// lets `i32 | void` through to emit, so this file pins the emitter's BACKSTOP wording for a
// shape that a complete checker would never forward. A reject-parity pin: were the checker
// hole closed, this case would move to the `@error` tier and its wording here would go with
// it, which is the intended direction of travel, not a regression.
function sideeffect() { print(9) }
function f(c: boolean) { return if c { 1 } else { sideeffect() } }
print(f(true))
